## Keep the native route

Use the native Energy and History panels. The optional companion changes how supported Energy charts look. Theme YAML alone cannot do this. A dashboard summary links to the native detail. It does not replace it or hide what it can do.

The adapter covers seven graph cards inside Energy, against Home Assistant frontend 20261028.5. History is not covered by those Energy selectors and stays native. The [working examples](../components/chart-examples.html) show the adapter with synthetic data.

Home Assistant fetches the data, aggregates it, converts units and calculates costs. Do not add sensor polling, and do not write to an energy entity to make a demonstration.

## Show energy over a period as stacked columns

Energy used in each interval is a quantity per interval, such as kWh. Use vertical stacked columns, with time running left to right. Start the vertical scale at zero and keep each segment's native stack and sign.

Use the interval and unit Home Assistant provides. Do not scale totals to 100%, merge small sources into an invented “other” series, or combine import and export. Keep the native comparison and forecast series. The table must show the same period, series and values as the chart (WG-077).

Segments inside a stack do not share a baseline, so they are harder to compare. Label each source and keep the exact values available. A stack that relies on colour alone is not accessible.

## Show power over time as lines

Power is a rate, in W or kW. Never label it as energy (WG-070). Use one unfilled line for each source, with a matching key and quiet horizontal gridlines.

Lines are 1.5px wide, including on hover and in the legend key, because Power data is dense. The line width is read from `ENERGY_CHART_SPEC.lineWidth` in the adapter. Do not add gradient fills, cumulative stacking or smoothing to Power. Each line shows that source's unchanged value at that time. The total line keeps its neutral colour and a distinct dash.

When Home Assistant supplies a verified positive and negative pair, join them into one signed line from the existing signed samples. Keep the native arrays and IDs so the native view can be restored, and use the same signed values in View data.

Do not smooth away peaks, shift timestamps or fill a missing reading with zero. A missing reading is not zero (WG-076). Show a gap as a gap.

## Leave the distribution diagram native

The distribution diagram is a Sankey-style flow. The adapter does not change it and adds no data table for it. Keep its nodes, edges, quantities and interactions. Do not turn the flows into a stacked bar to fit a chart style.

Offer a text alternative only when the diagram exposes the values reliably. Say what it contains: a table of node values is not a table of flows from source to destination. Never work out edge values from node totals, and never count the same energy twice. See [Energy flow and other chart families](../home-assistant/energy-flow-and-other-charts.html) for the native behaviour.

Do not describe the column and line work as a replacement for the distribution diagram or as an accessibility audit of it (WG-086).

## Give every value its unit, period and time

Show the unit and the period beside each value (WG-073, WG-074). Say whether a value is an instant reading, a period total or an estimate. Do not convert a missing reading to zero.

Keep the tariff, currency, number format, time zone and units from Home Assistant. Keep the native previous and next controls, the day, week, month, year or custom range, and the comparison. The adapter adds no date picker of its own.

Show the selected period. Do not hard-code “today” when the person can change the date. Keep loading, unavailable, incomplete-period and no-data states. Update the table when the chart updates, and do not leave old values under a chart that is loading.

## Keep series colours stable

Use the [chart colours](../styles/chart-colour.html) for energy families and native device identities. Colours stay the same when the theme colour changes. Current and comparison columns are opaque. Hatching marks the previous period. Keep the native source names and legend state, and show values in text, because colour alone cannot separate every combination.

## Check the behaviour before release

1. Open native Energy in a fresh session with the companion loaded. Check the chart options and a screenshot, not only the theme variables.
2. Compare series count, names, values, timestamps, units, stacks and period before and after styling. Include zero, missing, negative and comparison values.
3. Change the date, open the data, use the legend and tooltips, and follow the native detail links. Check that the chart and the table agree each time.
4. Check columns, power history and distribution separately. A better column chart does not cover the distribution diagram.
5. Check light and dark modes, several theme colours, the keyboard, a 320px viewport and enlarged text. Keep wide tables inside their container.
6. Switch away from Civica, leave Energy and come back. Check that styling returns, with no duplicate tables or legends.
7. Say which checks used synthetic data and which used a live installation. One screenshot does not show that every custom chart card works or that a screen reader audit is done (WG-086, WG-095).

The [chart source record](../reference/ons-chart-sources.json) lists the reviewed upstream code and design decisions. The adapter depends on private Home Assistant elements, so it can break when the frontend changes. If an expected interface changes, keep the native behaviour.
